  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1043 AM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nACTIVE THIS WEEKEND:\n\nA line of showers and isolated thunderstorms along the leading \nedge of a shortwave are moving through eastern Utah and \nprogressing into western Colorado this morning, which models are\nnot picking up on or initializing well with. This line was \nfairly active through the early morning hours in eastern Utah. \nThis is likely due to added upper level support from a low \npressure trough moving into the Pacific Northwest, allowing the \nflow to shift to more southerly and therefore pull up deeper \nmonsoonal moisture last night into this morning and act on the \ninstability already in place. This trough is also nudging the \nhigh pressure ridge eastward. Deterministic models are fairly \nconsistent in showing this deeper moisture plume working its way\nacross our CWA today with PWAT anomalies jumping to 160 to 180 \npercent of normal. Where the models differ is in terms of how \nrobust or widespread the convection will be. The GFS is not as \nwidespread but the hi-res CAMs, particularly the HRRR, seem to \nindicate a later start to afternoon convection likely due to \nthis morning convection and cloud cover. After this morning wave\nmoves eastward, afternoon convection looks to refire off the \nhigher terrain, with coverage and more robust convection \nincreasing late this afternoon into the evening hours as a \nrobust shortwave moves through the flow providing added forcing \nand support. CAPE will be sufficient with around 500 to 800 J/kg\nwith mixing ratios in the 5 to 6 g/kg range which is sufficient\nfor heavy rain potential with any storms today. With the better\nsteering flow, there is a good chance storms will impact some \nlower valleys as well late in the day. The deeper moisture in \nplace today with the added forcing provided by the robust \nshortwave will increase flash flooding and debris flow concerns,\nespecially over burn scars and susceptible terrain. Be weather \naware and make sure you have a way to receive watches and \nwarnings. Cannot rule out some convection continuing through the\novernight hours but most activity should wind down by midnight \nor shortly after. \n\nOn Sunday, the Pacific Northwest trough will have lifted out \ninto Canada with the robust shortwave already out into the \nPlains, leaving us in a westerly flow as the shortwave and \ntrough allow the high to lay over. We will still see residual \nmoisture in place with another day of afternoon showers and \nstorms, but without the added forcing, storms should be mostly \nconfined to the high terrain. Flash flooding is still a concern \nover burn scars but looks to remain more localized on Sunday as \nthere isn't a real focus for where the better chances reside. \nHighs this weekend will be cooler than previous few days with \ntemperatures around 5 degrees cooler but still remaining 5 \ndegrees above normal.\n\nNEXT WEEK:\n\nThe high pressure and above normal moisture will remain in \nplace through this coming week with the high slowly meandering \nback to the west across the Desert Southwest (south of the Four \nCorners) by mid to late week. Some shortwaves will move through \nthe flow across the north up and around the high pressure \nthrough the Northern Rockies, providing a little lift for some \nafternoon convection each day but the focus seems to be over the\nhigh terrain with quite a bit of uncertainty as to the quality \nof this moisture with the heavy rain potential looking more \nlocalized as this moisture gets recycled under the high. PWAT \nanomalies slowly trend downward as this high shifts westward and\ndrier air is pulled into the Great Basin. Daytime highs hold \nsteady as we move into the coming week with highs around 5 \ndegrees above normal and gradually increase towards around 5 to \n10 degrees above normal by late week.",
